                <pb facs="0634" n="510" sameAs="frus:frus1881/pg510"/>

                <head>No. 308.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Blaine to Mr. Lowell</hi>.</head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Telegram.]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1881-03-08"><hi rendition="#italic">March</hi> 8,
                        1881</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>Evarts’ remark about advertising for claims was made to show British minister the
                    impracticability of sacrificing the rights of unknown parties who might
                    afterwards appear with just claims. At present no such claims are believed to
                    exist, but I could not consent in settling the known claims of one class of
                    persons to bar the claims of another class without notice.</p>
                <p>The simple question is whether Lord Granville is willing to adjust the claims
                    comprehended in 109 and 110, either by payment of lump sum or by reference of
                    the whole matter to British minister and myself. I am ready to consider either
                    mode of adjustment.</p>
                <closer><signed>BLAINE.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
